from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Technical writing is best described as...","options":["persuasive advertising copy","writing that helps a reader understand or do something","personal creative storytelling","formal academic argument for its own sake"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Technical writing serves a reader who needs to understand a topic or complete a task - see ''What technical writing is and how it differs.''"},{"prompt":"The single most important thing to establish before you write is...","options":["your word count","who the reader is and what they need to do","the font","the file format"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Audience and purpose shape every later choice - see ''Audience and purpose analysis.''"},{"prompt":"Plain language mainly means...","options":["using the longest precise word available","writing so the intended reader understands on the first read","removing all technical terms","writing only very short sentences"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plain language is about reader comprehension, not dumbing down - see ''Plain language and concision.''"},{"prompt":"Which is the clearest way to present eight sequential setup steps?","options":["one long paragraph","a numbered list","a bulleted list in random order","a wide table"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Actions that must happen in order belong in a numbered list - see ''Structuring documents.''"},{"prompt":"A README in a software project usually...","options":["lists every bug ever filed","tells a newcomer what the project is and how to start using it","replaces the source code","stores user passwords"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A README orients a new reader and gets them running quickly - see ''Common document types.''"},{"prompt":"A good step in a set of instructions begins with...","options":["a justification","the result you hope for","an imperative verb naming the action","an apology"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each step should start with a command verb like ''Click'' or ''Enter'' - see ''Writing step-by-step instructions and visuals.''"},{"prompt":"A style guide exists mainly to...","options":["slow writers down","keep terminology and formatting consistent across a team","replace editing","make documents longer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Consistency reduces reader confusion and is the core job of a style guide - see ''Style, editing, and the documentation process.''"},{"prompt":"Accessible documentation includes things like...","options":["color as the only way to signal meaning","descriptive alternative text for images","tiny low-contrast fonts","mouse-only navigation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alt text and strong contrast let more readers use your docs - see ''Style, editing, and the documentation process.''"}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What technical writing is and how it differs', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to define technical writing in plain terms and explain how it differs from marketing copy, academic argument, and fiction. You will learn to judge a document by four practical qualities - accuracy, clarity, attention to audience, and usability - rather than by how impressive it sounds. Technical writing is the work of explaining how something works or how to do something so that a specific reader can act with confidence and with as little friction as possible. It shows up everywhere: the setup card in a product box, the help article you search at midnight, the onboarding guide a new hire reads on day one. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you were asked what single quality separates a usable instruction from a merely correct one. Hold that thought. Correctness is the floor, not the goal; a step can be perfectly true and still useless if the reader cannot find it, parse it, or map it onto the screen in front of them. By the end you should be able to look at any page and name which of the four qualities it is missing."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Technical writing is reader-centered and task-centered. Unlike an essay, whose job is to advance an argument, or an advertisement, whose job is to persuade, a technical document succeeds only when the reader understands or accomplishes something. That difference drives everything. Four qualities define the craft. Accuracy means every statement matches how the product or process actually behaves; a wrong value or an outdated screenshot destroys trust instantly. Clarity means the intended reader understands on the first read, without decoding jargon or rereading a tangled sentence. Audience awareness means you write for the knowledge, goals, and context of a particular reader, not for yourself or for an imaginary expert. Usability means the document works in the moment of need - the reader can scan it, find the relevant part, and act. Fiction and literary prose reward ambiguity, voice, and surprise; technical writing rewards the opposite: predictability, plain structure, and the absence of surprise. The reader is usually busy, often stressed, and rarely reading for pleasure. Your success is measured not by admiration but by whether they finished the task and did not have to ask for help."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Start by naming the reader and the task in one sentence before you write anything else: ''A first-time user wants to connect the device to home internet.'' That sentence keeps you honest. Compare two versions of the same instruction. Weak: ''Connectivity may be established through the appropriate configuration interface.'' Strong: ''Open Settings, tap Wi-Fi, and choose your network.'' The strong version is accurate, clear, written for a real reader, and usable at a glance. Notice what changed: a vague noun (''connectivity'') became concrete verbs (''open, tap, choose''), and the passive construction became a direct command. Another test is the midnight test - imagine the reader meets your page while tired and frustrated. Would they find the answer in ten seconds? A third technique is to separate concepts from tasks: explain what a setting does in one place, and how to change it in another, so a reader in a hurry is not forced to read theory. Finally, prefer the specific over the general. ''Wait a few moments'' invites doubt; ''Wait about thirty seconds until the light turns green'' tells the reader exactly when they have succeeded and removes the anxiety of not knowing."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In one sentence, define technical writing without using the word ''technical.'' 2) Name the four qualities covered in this lesson. 3) Rewrite ''Utilization of the apparatus should be preceded by activation'' as a plain instruction. 4) Is a product landing page technical writing? Why or why not? 5) Which quality is missing when a guide is perfectly accurate but readers still cannot find the step they need? Answers: 1) Writing that helps a specific reader understand or do something with as little friction as possible. 2) Accuracy, clarity, audience awareness, and usability. 3) ''Turn on the device before you use it.'' 4) Mostly no - its main job is to persuade a visitor to buy, not to help them complete a task, so it leans on marketing rather than instruction, though it may contain small technical parts. 5) Usability - the content is correct but the reader cannot locate or act on it, so the document still fails its job."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: writing to impress rather than to inform, which fills a page with long words and empty qualifiers while the reader''s question goes unanswered. Mistake: confusing correctness with usefulness - a manual can be entirely true and still fail because nobody can navigate it. Mistake: writing for yourself, the expert who already knows where everything is, instead of for the newcomer who does not. Mistake: burying the one sentence the reader actually needs under three paragraphs of background. The fix for all four is the same discipline: name your reader and their task first, then keep only what serves them. Exit ticket: (a) Name the four qualities that define technical writing. (b) A set of instructions is accurate but readers keep getting lost in it - which quality is it failing? Answers: (a) accuracy, clarity, audience awareness, and usability; (b) usability, since correct content that a reader cannot find or follow does not help them finish the task."}]}'::jsonb, 'Prefer a concrete verb to an abstract noun: ''choose your network'' beats ''network selection should occur.''', 12),
  (3, 'Audience and purpose analysis', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to analyze an audience and a purpose before you write, and let that analysis drive every decision that follows. You will build a short reader profile, state the document''s purpose in one sentence, and use both to decide what to include, what to assume, and what to leave out. Most weak documents are not badly written sentence by sentence; they are aimed at the wrong reader, or at no particular reader at all. The same facts about a database can become a one-paragraph note for an experienced engineer or a step-by-step tutorial for a brand-new user, and both can be excellent - but only if you knew which one you were writing. Warm-up: picture two readers for a single feature, an expert and a novice, and ask what each already knows, what each is trying to do, and where each will be reading. By the end you will treat ''who is this for and why'' as the first question of every project, not an afterthought you patch in later."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Audience analysis means forming a clear picture of the people who will actually read your document. Ask what they already know, what they are trying to accomplish, what vocabulary they share, and the conditions under which they read - rushed, distracted, on a small screen, or under pressure. Purpose analysis means stating, in one sentence, what the document should let the reader do or decide. A purpose is a verb, not a topic: not ''the billing system'' but ''help an account owner update a payment method without support.'' Together, audience and purpose set the scope. They tell you how much background to give, which terms to define, how long the document should be, and what to cut. A common framing divides readers by expertise - novice, intermediate, expert - and by role - end user, administrator, developer, decision-maker. A novice needs definitions and complete steps; an expert needs a fast reference and will resent being told what they already know. When a document must serve several readers, you can layer it: a quick summary up top for the hurried, with deeper detail below for those who want it."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Write a one-line reader profile and pin it above your draft: ''Reader: a small-business owner, not a developer, setting up online payments for the first time, probably on a laptop, wants to be done in ten minutes.'' Then write the purpose: ''Let this reader accept their first card payment today.'' Every later choice flows from those two lines. For that reader you would define the term ''gateway'' on first use, show exact buttons, and skip the configuration options that only developers touch. For a developer reader, you would drop the definitions, link to a reference, and lead with code. A useful technique is the knowledge gap check: list what the reader already knows, list what they must know to succeed, and the difference is exactly what your document must teach - no more. Another technique is to write the reader''s question as a heading, because readers arrive with questions, not topics: ''How do I refund a customer?'' beats ''Refund functionality.'' Finally, test scope by asking of each paragraph, ''Does my named reader need this to reach the purpose?'' If not, cut it or move it somewhere else."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write a one-line reader profile for a guide on filing an expense report at a new job. 2) State that guide''s purpose as a single verb-driven sentence. 3) You are documenting a feature for both novices and experts in one page - name one layering technique that serves both. 4) True or false: a clear purpose can justify cutting accurate, interesting content. 5) A reader keeps asking ''but what does that word mean?'' - what did your audience analysis miss? Answers: 1) Example - ''A new employee, not in finance, filing their first expense report, on a work laptop, wants reimbursement approved quickly.'' 2) ''Help a new employee submit a complete, approvable expense report on the first try.'' 3) Put a short summary and quick steps up top for experts, with lower, expandable detail and definitions for novices below. 4) True - if content does not serve the reader and purpose, interesting is not enough, so move or cut it. 5) The shared vocabulary - you assumed knowledge of a term the reader does not have, so it needed defining on first use."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: writing for yourself, the person who built the thing and forgot what it felt like not to know. Mistake: aiming at everyone at once, which produces a document too basic for experts and too terse for beginners. Mistake: confusing topic with purpose - ''the settings page'' describes subject matter but says nothing about what the reader should be able to do. Mistake: skipping the analysis because you are in a hurry, which guarantees rework when the document misses its reader. The discipline that prevents all of these is cheap: two sentences, a reader profile and a purpose, written before the draft and kept in view while you write. Exit ticket: (a) What two things should you define before writing anything else? (b) Rewrite the topic ''password reset'' as a purpose statement. Answers: (a) the audience, as a short reader profile, and the purpose, as one verb-driven sentence; (b) for example, ''Let a locked-out user reset their password and sign back in without contacting support.''"}]}'::jsonb, 'State a purpose as a verb the reader performs, not a noun naming a feature.', 12),
  (4, 'Plain language and concision', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson gives you the core moves of plain language: writing so your intended reader understands on the first read, and cutting words that carry no meaning. You will learn to prefer short common words over long rare ones, the active voice over the passive, and concrete actions over abstract nouns, and you will learn to measure a sentence by whether it helps the reader rather than by how formal it feels. Plain language is not dumbing down and it is not banning technical terms; a database administrator still needs the word ''index.'' It means removing the needless difficulty that sits on top of the necessary difficulty - the empty throat-clearing, the hedging, the fog. Warm-up: read any sentence you wrote today and try to delete three words without losing meaning. You almost always can. By the end you will be able to take a bloated, official-sounding paragraph and rewrite it so it is shorter, clearer, and more accurate at once, and you will understand why those three improvements tend to arrive together rather than in conflict."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Concision means every word earns its place. Plain language means the reader, not the writer, is the judge of clarity. The two reinforce each other: cutting clutter usually makes meaning sharper, and choosing the plain word usually makes the sentence shorter. A few reliable principles carry most of the work. First, prefer the active voice: ''the server rejects the request'' is shorter and clearer than ''the request is rejected by the server,'' and it names who acts. Second, turn abstract nouns back into verbs: ''make a decision'' becomes ''decide,'' ''perform an installation'' becomes ''install.'' This move, sometimes called undoing nominalization, deflates stiff prose fast. Third, cut empty phrases: ''in order to'' is just ''to,'' ''at this point in time'' is ''now,'' ''due to the fact that'' is ''because.'' Fourth, keep one main idea per sentence, and keep sentences short enough to hold in one breath. Fifth, use the reader''s words, not insider shorthand, and define a term the first time it appears. None of this removes precision; it removes the packaging around precision so the reader reaches the meaning faster."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Rewrite by subtraction. Take ''It is recommended that users should endeavor to ensure that all required fields have been completed prior to submission.'' Cut the hedging and the padding: ''Fill in all required fields before you submit.'' Twenty-two words became eight, and the instruction is clearer and more direct. Another example of undoing a nominalization: ''The utilization of the backup functionality is a requirement'' becomes ''You must use the backup.'' Watch for signal phrases that almost always mark clutter: ''there is'' or ''there are'' openings, ''the fact that,'' ''in terms of,'' and strings of nouns stacked together like ''user access permission configuration settings.'' Unstack them into a short phrase with a verb. A practical routine: write your draft freely, then pass through it once hunting only for words to delete, and a second time converting passive to active and nouns to verbs. Read the result aloud; anywhere you stumble or run out of breath marks a sentence to split. Keep a short personal list of your own habitual filler - everyone has a few - and search for them before you call a draft done."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Shorten ''in the event that the connection is lost'' to one word plus the clause. 2) Convert to active voice: ''The file was deleted by the system.'' 3) Undo the nominalization: ''perform a review of the logs.'' 4) Cut the filler: ''At this point in time, we are currently in the process of updating.'' 5) Is ''utilize'' ever better than ''use''? 6) Rewrite ''there are many users who report errors'' without the empty opening. Answers: 1) ''If the connection is lost.'' 2) ''The system deleted the file.'' 3) ''Review the logs.'' 4) ''We are updating'' - ''at this point in time,'' ''currently,'' and ''in the process of'' all say ''now'' and can go. 5) Almost never for general readers; ''use'' is shorter and clearer, so reserve ''utilize'' only where it carries a genuinely distinct technical sense, which is rare. 6) ''Many users report errors.''"},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: equating formality with quality, so the writer reaches for ''endeavor,'' ''utilize,'' and ''commence'' when ''try,'' ''use,'' and ''start'' would serve the reader better. Mistake: over-cutting until accuracy suffers - concision removes empty words, never necessary qualifications, so ''delete the file'' must not lose a genuine ''only if you have a backup.'' Mistake: treating the passive voice as forbidden; it is occasionally the right choice when the actor is unknown or irrelevant, as in ''the account was locked after five failed attempts.'' Mistake: banning all technical terms, which can leave the real reader underserved. The balance to aim for is plain but not simplistic, short but still complete. Exit ticket: (a) Name two reliable moves that make a sentence more concise. (b) Rewrite ''A determination will be made by the committee'' in the active voice. Answers: (a) for example, cut empty phrases like ''in order to,'' and convert abstract nouns back into verbs; preferring active voice and short common words also helps; (b) ''The committee will decide.''"}]}'::jsonb, 'Prefer the active voice by default; reach for the passive only when the actor is unknown or does not matter.', 13),
  (5, 'Structuring documents: headings, chunking, lists, tables', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson is about shape: how to organize a document so a reader can scan it, find the one part they need, and skip the rest. You will learn to use descriptive headings, to chunk content into short labeled sections, and to choose the right container for each kind of information - a paragraph, a numbered list, a bulleted list, or a table. Readers of technical documents almost never read start to finish; they scan, jump, and hunt. Structure is what makes that possible. Warm-up: think of the last help page you used. Did you read every word, or did you scan the headings, land on one section, and act? By the end of this lesson you will be able to take an undifferentiated wall of text and reshape it into something a hurried reader can navigate in seconds - breaking it into sections with informative headings, pulling parallel items into lists, and moving row-and-column data into a table where the reader can compare across it at a glance."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Good structure matches the shape of the content to the shape of the reader''s need. Headings are signposts: they let a reader scan a page and jump to the relevant part, so they must describe content, not decorate it - ''Reset your password'' tells the reader where they are, while ''Introduction'' does not. Chunking means breaking long text into short, self-contained sections, each doing one job, because a reader can hold a small labeled block in mind far more easily than a long undivided passage. Lists expose parallel items that a paragraph would hide. Use a numbered list when order matters, as in sequential steps, and a bulleted list when items are parallel but unordered, as in a set of options or requirements. Tables are for information with a repeating structure - several items that each share the same attributes - so the reader can compare down a column or across a row. The underlying principle is called progressive disclosure: put the most important or most common information first and near the top, and let detail and edge cases come later, so the typical reader succeeds quickly and the unusual reader can still dig deeper."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Turn a buried procedure into a numbered list. Before: ''First open the console, then you will want to select the project, after which you click Deploy and confirm.'' After, as four numbered steps, each starting with a verb: ''Open the console,'' ''Select the project,'' ''Click Deploy,'' ''Confirm.'' The reader can now count the steps and track their place. Use a bulleted list for parallel, unordered items: the three things you need before you start, or the four supported file types. Use a table when items share attributes - comparing three pricing plans across price, seats, and support becomes a three-column table the reader can scan down, where a paragraph would force them to hold every number in memory. Write headings as the reader''s questions or as verb phrases: ''How do I cancel?'' or ''Cancel a subscription,'' not ''Cancellation.'' Keep headings parallel in grammar so a scan reads smoothly. And front-load: put the answer or the most common path first, with rare exceptions below, so the reader who wants the usual case is not made to wade through the unusual one to reach it."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Order matters or not: the steps to install software - numbered or bulleted list? 2) Order matters or not: the system requirements - numbered or bulleted list? 3) You are comparing four laptops across price, weight, and battery life - what structure fits best? 4) Rewrite the heading ''Overview'' so it actually describes a section about connecting to Wi-Fi. 5) What is progressive disclosure, in one sentence? Answers: 1) A numbered list, because the steps must be done in sequence. 2) A bulleted list, because the requirements are parallel and have no order. 3) A table, with one row per laptop and one column per attribute, so the reader can compare across them. 4) For example, ''Connect to Wi-Fi.'' 5) Showing the most important or most common information first, and revealing detail and edge cases progressively, so typical readers succeed fast while others can still go deeper."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: vague headings like ''Introduction,'' ''Overview,'' or ''Information'' that tell a scanning reader nothing about what is inside. Mistake: using a bulleted list for steps that must happen in order, which hides the sequence the reader depends on. Mistake: cramming comparative, attribute-heavy data into prose instead of a table, forcing the reader to hold numbers in their head. Mistake: one giant undivided block of text with no sections, so there is nothing to scan and no way in. Mistake: burying the most common case beneath rare exceptions, so the typical reader pays for the unusual one. The cure is to ask, for each piece of content, what shape this information has, and then to pick the container that matches it. Exit ticket: (a) When should you use a numbered list instead of a bulleted list? (b) What makes a heading useful to someone scanning the page? Answers: (a) when the order of the items matters, such as sequential steps; (b) it describes the content specifically, so the reader can tell at a glance whether the section holds what they need."}]}'::jsonb, 'A numbered list signals sequence; a bulleted list signals parallel items with no required order.', 12),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Compared with a persuasive essay, technical writing prioritizes...","options":["emotional appeal","accuracy, clarity, and usability","ambiguity","the writer''s personal voice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A technical document succeeds only when the reader understands or does something, so it leans on accuracy, clarity, and usability."},{"prompt":"Writing the same install guide for a beginner and for an expert should differ mainly in...","options":["the programming language","the amount of assumed knowledge and detail","the truth of the facts","nothing at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Audience analysis changes scope and detail, not the underlying facts."},{"prompt":"Which rewrite is more concise with the same meaning: ''At this point in time we are in the process of testing'' becomes...","options":["''We test now and then''","''We are now testing''","''Testing is a process that occurs''","''The testing process is underway at this time''"],"answer":1,"explanation":"''At this point in time'' and ''in the process of'' both reduce to ''now,'' giving ''We are now testing.''"},{"prompt":"Breaking a long wall of text into short labeled sections is called...","options":["padding","chunking","indexing","compiling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chunking makes content scannable by splitting it into small, self-contained blocks."},{"prompt":"Information with several items that each share the same attributes is best shown as...","options":["a paragraph","a numbered list","a table","a single long sentence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A table lets the reader compare down a column or across a row at a glance."},{"prompt":"Good headings should...","options":["be vague so they fit anything","describe the content so readers can scan and find","all be identical","be hidden from the reader"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Descriptive headings act as signposts for a scanning reader - ''Reset your password,'' not ''Introduction.''"}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Common document types', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson maps the common genres of technical documentation so you can recognize which one a task calls for and what each reader expects from it. You will be able to tell instructions from a user guide, a reference from a tutorial, release notes from a README, and a report from a proposal, and to match the right form to the reader''s need. Each genre carries its own conventions, and readers arrive with expectations shaped by them: someone opening a reference wants to look up one method quickly, while someone opening a getting-started guide wants to be led by the hand. Choosing the wrong genre is a common and costly error - answering a quick-reference need with a long narrative tutorial frustrates everyone. Warm-up: think of three documents you have used this week and name what each was for. By the end you will be able to look at a documentation task and say which genre fits, so you can set the right scope, structure, and tone from the first line instead of discovering the mismatch after you have written it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Technical documents cluster into a handful of recurring types, each answering a different reader question. Instructions, or procedures, answer ''how do I do this task?'' with ordered steps. A user guide is broader, explaining a whole product feature by feature for everyday use. Reference documentation answers ''what exactly is this?'' - it is comprehensive, structured for lookup rather than reading through, and includes things like interface references, option lists, and command references. A tutorial teaches by leading a newcomer through a complete worked example from start to finish. Release notes tell existing users what changed in a version: new features, fixes, and anything that breaks. A README is the front door of a software project, orienting a newcomer to what it is, how to install it, and how to start. Reports present findings or status to inform a decision, and proposals argue for a course of action and ask for approval or resources. A helpful way to organize these is a split many teams use: tutorials and how-to guides are learning- and task-oriented, while reference and explanation are information- and understanding-oriented. Knowing the type fixes scope, structure, and tone before you write."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Match the genre to the need with a quick test: is the reader trying to learn, to do, to look up, or to decide? Learning points to a tutorial; doing points to instructions or a how-to; looking up points to reference; deciding points to a report or proposal. Examples make the contrast concrete. ''Install the toolkit'' is instructions - numbered steps, verbs, one happy path. ''Toolkit User Guide'' is a user guide - feature by feature, broader, assuming repeated use. A reference entry lists a method, its parameters, return values, and errors in a predictable template, so a developer can scan straight to the parameter they forgot. Release notes read as a dated, bulleted list grouped into Added, Changed, Fixed, and a clearly marked Breaking changes section so upgraders are not surprised. A README opens with one sentence on what the project is, then install and quick-start, then links to deeper docs. A report leads with findings and a recommendation up front, details below. A proposal states the problem, the proposed solution, the cost, and the decision being requested. Use the template the genre expects and your reader feels instantly at home."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A user forgot the parameters of one function and wants to look them up fast - which genre? 2) A brand-new user wants to be walked through building their first project from start to finish - which genre? 3) You need to tell existing customers what changed in version 2.0, including one breaking change - which genre, and what must you clearly mark? 4) Someone lands on your open-source project''s page for the first time - which document greets them, and what are its first two jobs? 5) You want leadership to approve budget for a new tool - report or proposal? Answers: 1) Reference, such as interface docs, structured for lookup. 2) A tutorial, which leads a newcomer through a complete worked example. 3) Release notes, with a clearly labeled ''Breaking changes'' section so upgraders are warned. 4) A README, whose first jobs are to say what the project is and how to install and start it. 5) A proposal, since you are arguing for a course of action and requesting a decision and resources."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: answering a lookup need with a narrative - burying a method''s parameters inside a long tutorial, so the reader who wanted one fact has to read a story. Mistake: mixing genres in one document, such as weaving conceptual explanation through step-by-step instructions until neither the learner nor the doer is served. Mistake: release notes that list fixes but hide a breaking change in the middle, so upgraders discover it the hard way. Mistake: a README that dives into advanced configuration before telling a newcomer what the project even is. Mistake: writing a long report when a one-page proposal with a clear ask was what the decision-maker needed. The remedy is to decide the genre first - learn, do, look up, or decide - and hold to its conventions. Exit ticket: (a) What is the core difference between reference documentation and a tutorial? (b) What must release notes always make unmistakable? Answers: (a) reference is comprehensive and built for quick lookup, while a tutorial leads a newcomer through a complete worked example to learn; (b) any breaking changes, flagged clearly so upgrading readers are not caught out."}]}'::jsonb, 'Ask whether the reader wants to learn, do, look up, or decide - the answer names the genre.', 13),
  (8, 'Writing step-by-step instructions and visuals', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson teaches the single most common technical-writing task - writing instructions people can actually follow - together with the visuals that support them. You will be able to write numbered steps that each begin with an action verb, that state where to act before what to do, that give the reader a way to confirm success, and that handle what happens when something goes wrong. You will also learn when a picture genuinely helps and how to design one that clarifies rather than decorates. Instructions fail in predictable ways: steps that bundle three actions together, steps that assume the reader already knows where a button is, steps that never tell the reader how to know they succeeded. Warm-up: recall a time you followed instructions and got lost - what did the step fail to tell you? By the end you will be able to take a messy procedure and turn it into clean, testable steps, and to decide for each one whether a screenshot, a diagram, or no image at all serves the reader best."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A good instruction is a sequence of single actions, each one testable. The core rules are few and strong. One action per step: if a step contains the word ''and'' joining two actions, split it. Start each step with an imperative verb - ''Click,'' ''Enter,'' ''Select'' - so the reader knows immediately what to do. State location before action: ''In the toolbar, click Save,'' not ''Click Save, which is in the toolbar,'' because the reader looks where you point before they act. Tell the reader the expected result when it is not obvious: ''A confirmation message appears,'' so they can tell the step worked. Number the steps, because sequence is the whole point, and keep each step short. Before the steps, state any prerequisites - what the reader needs and any assumptions - so nobody gets halfway and discovers a missing requirement. Handle failure where it happens, with a brief note on what to do if the expected result does not appear. Visuals follow the same reader-first logic: an image earns its place only when it shows something words cannot say as quickly - a location on a crowded screen, a physical connection, a flow. A decorative image is just noise."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"Rewrite a bundled step. Before: ''Open the file menu and select Export and then choose PDF and click Save.'' After, as four numbered steps: ''Open the File menu. Select Export. Choose PDF. Click Save.'' Each is one action the reader can perform and check. Add location and result where they help: ''In the top-left corner, click the gear icon. The Settings panel opens.'' Now the reader knows where to look and how to confirm success. State prerequisites first: ''Before you start, you need an account and the desktop app installed.'' Handle the unhappy path inline: ''If the light stays red, unplug the cable, wait ten seconds, and reconnect.'' For visuals, prefer a cropped, annotated screenshot that highlights the one button in question over a full-screen capture where the reader must hunt. Use a simple diagram when you need to show how parts relate or how data flows, and a callout or numbered marker to tie an image back to its step. Keep images current - an outdated screenshot is worse than none, because it actively misleads. And always provide descriptive alternative text so a reader who cannot see the image still gets the information it carries."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Split this into clean steps: ''Log in and go to settings and change your email and save.'' 2) Add a confirmation to the step ''Click Submit.'' 3) Fix the order within this step: ''Click Delete in the Account section.'' 4) What should you list before the first step of any procedure? 5) When does a screenshot earn its place, and what must always accompany it? Answers: 1) Four numbered steps - ''Log in. Go to Settings. Change your email. Save.'' 2) For example, ''Click Submit. A confirmation message appears.'' 3) Put location first - ''In the Account section, click Delete.'' 4) The prerequisites: what the reader needs and any assumptions, so they do not get stuck partway. 5) When it shows something words cannot convey as fast, such as the location of a control on a busy screen; it must always carry descriptive alternative text, and it must be kept up to date."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: bundling several actions into one step, so the reader loses their place or skips part of it. Mistake: putting the action before the location, which makes the reader act before they know where to look. Mistake: omitting the expected result, leaving the reader unsure whether a step worked and whether it is safe to continue. Mistake: forgetting prerequisites, so the reader reaches step four and learns they needed something from before step one. Mistake: decorative or outdated screenshots - an image that shows an old interface sends the reader looking for a button that moved. Mistake: images with no alternative text, which shut out readers who cannot see them. The fix is discipline: one action per step, location before action, a way to confirm success, prerequisites up front, and every visual chosen to inform. Exit ticket: (a) How many actions should a single instruction step contain? (b) Name two things every supporting screenshot needs. Answers: (a) one; (b) it must be current, and it must have descriptive alternative text - and ideally it is cropped and annotated to highlight the relevant control."}]}'::jsonb, 'Start each step with an imperative verb, and name the location before the action.', 14),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"audience","translation":"the specific people a document is written for, defined by what they know, what they want, and where they read","example":"Before writing, name your audience: a first-time user on a phone, not an engineer."},{"term":"plain language","translation":"writing so the intended reader understands on the first read, without needless difficulty","example":"Plain language turns ''utilize the apparatus'' into ''use the device.''"},{"term":"concision","translation":"using only the words that carry meaning, with no filler","example":"Concision cuts ''at this point in time'' down to ''now.''"},{"term":"chunking","translation":"breaking long text into short, self-contained, labeled sections a reader can scan","example":"Chunking a wall of text into headed blocks lets a reader jump straight to the part they need."},{"term":"progressive disclosure","translation":"showing the most common information first and revealing detail and edge cases later","example":"Progressive disclosure puts the quick answer up top and the rare exceptions below it."},{"term":"style guide","translation":"a shared agreement on terminology, tone, and formatting that keeps a team''s docs consistent","example":"The style guide says ''sign in,'' so nobody writes ''log in'' or ''login.''"},{"term":"usability testing","translation":"watching a real target reader try to use a document to do the task, and noting where they struggle","example":"Usability testing revealed that readers missed step three almost every time."},{"term":"docs-as-code","translation":"writing documentation as plain-text files kept in version control and shipped through the same pipeline as the software","example":"With docs-as-code, a feature change and its doc update ship in the same review."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (10, 'Style, editing, usability, accessibility, and the documentation process', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This final lesson covers the work that turns a decent draft into trustworthy documentation: keeping style and terminology consistent, editing in focused passes, testing whether readers can actually use what you wrote, making documents accessible to more people, and understanding the modern process and tools that keep docs alive. You will be able to explain what a style guide does and why consistency matters, run an edit as separate passes rather than one overwhelmed read, design a simple usability test, apply a few core accessibility practices, and describe the docs-as-code approach in plain terms. Good documentation is rarely written well in one sitting; it is drafted, then revised, tested, and maintained. Warm-up: think of a help page that clearly had not been updated since the product changed - what did that cost you as a reader? By the end you will see writing as one stage in a cycle, and you will have a short, repeatable routine for taking any draft through consistency, editing, testing, and accessibility before you publish it, and for keeping it correct afterward."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Consistency is a feature. When the same thing is always called by the same name, and headings, capitalization, and formatting follow the same rules, the reader spends their attention on the content instead of on noticing differences. A style guide is the shared agreement that makes this possible across a team - it fixes terminology, voice and tone, formatting, and how to handle things like numbers, dates, and interface labels; many teams adopt an established one and add a short project-specific word list on top. Tone in technical writing is usually plain, direct, and respectful, favoring the second person (''you'') and the present tense. Editing is a separate skill from drafting and works best in passes: one pass for structure and completeness, one for clarity and concision, one for correctness of facts and steps, and a final proofreading pass for grammar and spelling. Trying to do all of these at once does none of them well. Usability testing means watching a real reader of the target kind try to use your document to do the task, and noting exactly where they hesitate or fail. Accessibility means designing so more people can read and act, regardless of how they reach the page."},{"heading":"Techniques & examples","text":"For consistency, keep a one-page terminology list - decide ''sign in,'' not a mix of ''log in,'' ''login,'' and ''sign on,'' and use it everywhere. Adopt a known style guide for the big decisions and record only your exceptions. For editing, make the passes concrete: pass one asks ''is anything missing or out of order?''; pass two deletes filler and converts passive to active; pass three verifies every value, command, and step against the real product; pass four catches typos, ideally after a break or read aloud, since fresh eyes catch what tired ones skip. For usability testing, the cheapest useful version is to hand your instructions to one person from the target audience, ask them to perform the task without your help, and simply watch - every place they pause marks a flaw to fix. For accessibility, write descriptive alternative text for images, never rely on color alone to carry meaning, use real headings so assistive technology can navigate, write descriptive link text instead of ''click here,'' and keep contrast high. On process, docs-as-code means writing documentation in plain-text files kept in version control alongside the software, reviewed and published through the same pipeline, so the docs change in step with the product."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Your document uses ''log in,'' ''sign in,'' and ''login'' interchangeably - what tool fixes this, and how? 2) Name the four editing passes in order. 3) Design the simplest possible usability test for a set of setup instructions. 4) Give two concrete accessibility practices for a page with images and links. 5) In one sentence, what does docs-as-code mean? Answers: 1) A style guide or project terminology list - pick one term and apply it everywhere. 2) Structure and completeness; clarity and concision; factual and step correctness; proofreading. 3) Give the instructions to one person from the target audience, ask them to do the task unaided, and watch for every hesitation or failure. 4) For example, write descriptive alternative text for each image, and do not use color as the only way to signal meaning; using descriptive link text rather than ''click here'' also helps. 5) Writing documentation as plain-text files kept in version control and published through the same review-and-release pipeline as the code, so docs stay in step with the product."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: inconsistent terminology, where one feature wears three names across a document and the reader cannot tell whether they are the same thing. Mistake: editing in a single exhausted read, trying to fix structure, clarity, facts, and typos at once and catching few of them. Mistake: never watching a real reader use the document, so obvious stumbling points ship unnoticed - the writer, who knows the product, cannot see them. Mistake: treating accessibility as optional, which quietly shuts out readers who rely on alt text, headings, or sufficient contrast. Mistake: publishing documentation once and walking away, so it drifts out of date as the product changes and slowly becomes a liability. The antidote is a cycle: write, make it consistent, edit in passes, test it on a real reader, make it accessible, publish, and keep it current as things change. Exit ticket: (a) Why is editing done in separate passes rather than all at once? (b) What is the core idea of docs-as-code? Answers: (a) each pass has one focus - structure, clarity, correctness, proofreading - so you catch far more than in a single overloaded read; (b) keeping documentation in version control beside the code and shipping it through the same review-and-release process, so docs and product stay in sync."}]}'::jsonb, 'Pick one term per concept and use it everywhere; inconsistent names make readers wonder whether you mean different things.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a' and l.position = v.pos
);
